o from 47% to 35%.","points":["Black answers 20…Qd6: moving away uncovers the bishop on d7, which now hits the b5 pawn.","Better was 20.Ne5 (−0.29)."],"better":"20.Ne5 threatens Nxc6+, winning the queen for a knight. After 20…Bxe5 21.dxe5 d4 22.exd4 Qxg2 23.Qf3 Qxf3 24.Nxf3 Bc6, the queens come off (−0.29)."},{"ply":40,"name":"20…Qd6","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"20…Qd6! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Moves the attacked queen to safety.","Moving away uncovers the bishop on d7, which now hits the b5 pawn.","The next best move, 20…Qc8, would drop Black's winning chances from 65% to 52% (−0.20).","20…Qd6 gets the queen out of the way of bxc6.","White was threatening bxc6, winning the queen for a pawn. The queen steps away.","White answers 21.Qd1."]},{"ply":44,"name":"22…e5","cls":"mistake","color":"b","headline":"22…e5 is a mistake: Black's winning chances go from 70% to 58%.","points":["White answers 23.f4: a pawn break: it challenges the e5 pawn to open lines.","Better was 22…h5 (−2.27), which pushes a pawn towards the White king, to pry open lines in front of it."],"better":"22…h5 pushes the pawn storm towards the king and shores up the g4 pawn. Engine line: 23.Qd1 Bf6 24.Qc2 Rhg8 25.Rb4 Rc8 (−2.27)."},{"ply":45,"name":"23.dxe5","cls":"mistake","color":"w","headline":"23.dxe5 is a mistake: White's winning chances go from 42% to 27%.","points":["Black answers 23…Bxe5, which takes the pawn on e5.","After Bxe5, Black wins 1 point of material within 2 moves.","Better was 23.f4 (−0.87): a pawn break: it challenges the e5 pawn to open lines."],"better":"23.f4 threatens fxe5, winning a pawn. Engine line: 23…exd4 24.exd4 Rhe8 25.Qf2 Re7 26.Ra1 (−0.87)."},{"ply":59,"name":"30.Qd4","cls":"mistake","color":"w","headline":"30.Qd4 is a mistake: White's winning chances go from 38% to 27%.","points":["Black answers 30…Qxd4, which takes the queen on d4.","Better was 30.a6 (−1.32): a pawn break: it challenges the b7 pawn to open lines."],"better":"30.a6 wins a pawn, picking it up with 31.axb7. After 30…Rh7 31.axb7 Be8 32.b6 axb6 33.Ra3 Rxb7 34.Ra6, White is a pawn down (−1.32)."},{"ply":61,"name":"31.Nd2","cls":"mistake","color":"w","headline":"31.Nd2 is a mistake: White's winning chances go from 30% to 16%.","points":["Black answers 31…Qxd4, which takes the queen on d4.","Better was 31.Qf4+ (−2.35), which gives check, so the reply is forced."],"better":"31.Qf4+ gives check. Engine line: 31…Ka8 32.Rcc1 Rc8 33.b6 a6 34.Qd4 (−2.35)."},{"ply":96,"name":"48…Rxe6","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"48…Rxe6! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Takes the rook on e6.","Shores up the bishop on c6, which was under as much attack as it had defence (1 against 1).","The next best move, 48…Rb2, would drop Black's winning chances from 92% to 65% (−1.65).","48…Rxe6 trades off the attacked rook instead of losing it to Rxf6.","White was threatening Rxf6, winning a rook. The rook takes something with it instead.","Trades rooks: trading pieces when ahead brings the win closer."]}],"opening":{"name":"Queen's Pawn Game: London System","eco":"D02","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 7…Nh5: 14 moves in all, 7 by White and 7 by Black.","White left the book with 8.Bg3. In 24 games from this position, strong players played 8.Bg5 (42%), 8.dxc5 (29%) or 8.Be5 (21%).","The main line goes on 8.Bg5 h6 9.Bh4 g5 10.Ne5.","From here White scored 52% in those games, with 4% drawn."]}}